* @return Collection of surrounding namespaces.\n92:      */\n93:     Collection getAllSurroundingNamespaces(Object ns);\n94: \n95:     /**\n96:      * @param ns the given namespace\n97:      * @return a collection of all behavioralfeatures in the given namespace\n98:      */\n99:     Collection getAllBehavioralFeatures(Object ns);\n100: \n101:     /**\n102:      * Get the modelelement a given path below a given root-namespace.\n103:      *\n104:      * @param path the given path\n105:      * @param theRootNamespace the given namespace to start from\n106:      * @return the modelelement looked for, or null if not found\n107:      */\n108:     Object getElement(Vector path, Object theRootNamespace);\n109: \n110:     /**\n111:      * Finds the absolute path of a ModelElement. Ie the name of each\n112:      * namespace starting at the root (the Model) and ending with the\n113:      * name of the element.<p>\n114:      *\n115:      * The returned Vector implicitly starts at the root (the model)\n116:      * and follows element's chain of owning namespaces back down to\n117:      * element. The first element will thus be the name of the top level\n118:      * namespace below the model, and the last element will be the name\n119:      * of element itself. Note thus that for the model the path will be\n120:      * empty.\n121:      *\n122:      * @param  element is the object to resolve the path for.\n123:      * @return A Vector as described above.\n124:      * @throws IllegalArgumentException if element isn't a ModelElement\n125:      *         properly owned by namespaces and a model.\n126:      */\n127:     Vector getPath(Object element);\n128: \n129:     /**\n130:      * Utility function for managing several overlayed models, eg a user\n131:      * model to which elements from some profile models is imported when\n132:      * needed. This version of the function assumes it is permissible to\n133:      * create missing elements.\n134:      *\n135:      * This function may fail and return null eg if some required object\n136:      * doesn't exist in the target model and cannot be copied.\n137:      *\n138:      * @param elem is some element.\n139:      * @param model is the model the returned object shall belong to.\n140:      * @return An element of the same type and at the same position in the\n141:      *  model as elem, or if that would turn out impossible then null.\n142:      */\n143:     Object getCorrespondingElement(Object elem, Object model);\n144: \n145:     /**\n146:      * Utility function for managing several overlayed models, eg a user\n147:      * model to which elements from some profile models is imported when\n148:      * needed. This version of the function will only copy objects if\n149:      * canCreate is true, but may then also copy other missing elements.\n150:      *\n151:      * This function may fail and return null eg if the required object\n152:      * doesn't exist in the target model and canCreate is false or some\n153:      * required object doesn't exist in the target model and cannot be\n154:      * copied.\n155:      *\n156:      * @param elem is some element.\n157:      * @param model is the model the returned object shall belong to.\n158:      * @param canCreate determines if objects can be copied into model.\n159:      * @return An element of the same type and at the same position in the\n160:      *  model as elem, or if that would turn out impossible then null.\n161:      */\n162:     Object getCorrespondingElement(Object elem, Object model,\n163:             \t\t\t   boolean canCreate);\n164: \n165:     /**\n166:      * Tests if two objects are of the same type, have the same name and the\n167:      * same relative position in the model.\n168:      *\n169:      * Same relative position implies either:<ul>\n170:      * <li>their owners correspond to eachother.\n171:      * <li>they are both owned by model objects.\n172:      * </ul>\n173:      *\n174:      * @param obj1 is an object.\n175: 