espace starting at the root (the Model) and ending with the\n363:      * name of the element.<p>\n364:      *\n365:      * The returned Vector implicitly starts at the root (the model)\n366:      * and follows element's chain of owning namespaces back down to\n367:      * element. The first element will thus be the name of the top level\n368:      * namespace below the model, and the last element will be the name\n369:      * of element itself. Note thus that for the model the path will be\n370:      * empty.\n371:      *\n372:      * @param  element is the object to resolve the path for.\n373:      * @return A Vector as described above.\n374:      * @throws IllegalArgumentException if element isn't a ModelElement\n375:      *         properly owned by namespaces and a model.\n376:      */\n377:     public Vector getPath(Object element) {\n378:         Vector path;\n379: \n380:         if (ModelFacade.isAModel(element)) {\n381:             return new Vector();\n382:         }\n383: \n384:         path = getPath(ModelFacade.getNamespace(element));\n385:         path.add(ModelFacade.getName(element));\n386: \n387:         return path;\n388:     }\n389: \n390:     /**\n391:      * Utility function for managing several overlayed models, eg a user\n392:      * model to which elements from some profile models is imported when\n393:      * needed. This version of the function assumes it is permissible to\n394:      * create missing elements.\n395:      *\n396:      * This function may fail and return null eg if some required object\n397:      * doesn't exist in the target model and cannot be copied.\n398:      *\n399:      * @param elem is some element.\n400:      * @param model is the model the returned object shall belong to.\n401:      * @return An element of the same type and at the same position in the\n402:      *  model as elem, or if that would turn out impossible then null.\n403:      */\n404:     public Object getCorrespondingElement(Object elem, Object model) {\