                    "kwargs": {
                        "content": "Here is the source code to modify: \nsrc_new/org/argouml/uml/diagram/use_case/ui/FigUseCase.java: \n1: // Copyright (c) 1996-99 The Regents of the University of California. All\n2: // Rights Reserved. Permission to use, copy, modify, and distribute this\n3: // software and its documentation without fee, and without a written\n4: // agreement is hereby granted, provided that the above copyright notice\n5: // and this paragraph appear in all copies.  This software program and\n6: // documentation are copyrighted by The Regents of the University of\n7: // California. The software program and documentation are supplied \"AS\n8: // IS\", without any accompanying services from The Regents. The Regents\n9: // does not warrant that the operation of the program will be\n10: // uninterrupted or error-free. The end-user understands that the program\n11: // was developed for research purposes and is advised not to rely\n12: // exclusively on the program for any reason.  IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\n13: // UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA BE LIABLE TO ANY PARTY FOR DIRECT, INDIRECT,\n14: // SPECIAL, INCIDENTAL, OR CONSEQUENTIAL DAMAGES, INCLUDING LOST PROFITS,\n15: // ARISING OUT OF THE USE OF THIS SOFTWARE AND ITS DOCUMENTATION, EVEN IF\n16: // THE UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA HAS BEEN ADVISED OF THE POSSIBILITY OF\n17: // SUCH DAMAGE. THE UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA SPECIFICALLY DISCLAIMS ANY\n18: // WARRANTIES, INCLUDING, BUT NOT LIMITED TO, THE IMPLIED WARRANTIES OF\n19: // MERCHANTABILITY AND FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE. THE SOFTWARE\n20: // PROVIDED HEREUNDER IS ON AN \"AS IS\" BASIS, AND THE UNIVERSITY OF\n21: // CALIFORNIA HAS NO OBLIGATIONS TO PROVIDE MAINTENANCE, SUPPORT,\n22: // UPDATES, ENHANCEMENTS, OR MODIFICATIONS.\n23: \n24: // File: FigUseCase.java\n25: // Classes: FigUseCase\n26: // Original Author: your email address here\n27: // $Id$\n28: \n29: // 8 Apr 2002: Jeremy Bennett (mail@jeremybennett.com). Extended to support\n30: // the display of extension points.\n31: \n32: \n33: package org.argouml.uml.diagram.use_case.ui;\n34: \n35: import java.awt.*;\n36: import java.awt.event.*;\n37: import java.util.*;\n38: import java.beans.*;\n39: import javax.swing.*;\n40: \n41: import ru.novosoft.uml.foundation.core.*;\n42: import ru.novosoft.uml.behavior.use_cases.*;\n43: \n44: import org.tigris.gef.base.*;\n45: import org.tigris.gef.presentation.*;\n46: import org.tigris.gef.graph.*;\n47: \n48: import org.apache.log4j.Category;\n49: import org.argouml.application.api.*;\n50: import org.argouml.model.uml.UmlHelper;\n51: import org.argouml.ui.*;\n52: import org.argouml.uml.ui.*;\n53: import org.argouml.uml.diagram.ui.*;\n54: import org.argouml.uml.generator.*;\n55: \n56: \n57: /**\n58:  * <p>A fig to display use cases on use case diagrams.</p>\n59:  *\n60:  * <p>Realised as a solid oval containing the name of the use case. Optionally\n61:  *   may be split into two compartments, with the lower compartment displaying\n62:  *   the extension points for the use case.</p>\n63:  *\n64:  * <p>Implements all interfaces through its superclasses.</p>\n65:  *\n66:  * <p>There is some coordinate geometry to be done to fit rectangular text\n67:  *   boxes inside an elipse. The rectangular text box contains the name and any\n68:  *   extension points if shown, and is deemed to be of height <em>2h</em> and\n69:  *   width <em>2w</em>. We allow a margin of <em>p</em> above the top and below\n70:  *   the bottom of the box, so we know the height of the elipse, <em>2b</em> =\n71:  *   <em>2h</em> + <em>2p</em>.</p>\n72:  *\n73:  * <p>The formula for an elipse of width <em>2a</em> and height <em>2b</em>,\n74:  *   centred on the origin, is</p>\n75:  *\n76:  * <p><em>x</em>^2/<em>a</em>^2 + <em>y</em>^2/<em>b</em>^2 = 1.</p>\n77:  *\n78:  * <p>We know that a corner of the rectangle is at coordinate\n79:  *   (<em>w</em>,<em>h</em>), since the rectangle must also be centred on the\n80:  *   origin to fit within the elipse. Subsituting these values for <em>x</em>\n81:  *   and <em>y</em> in the formula above, we can compute <em>a</em>, half the\n82:  *   width of the elipse, since we know <em>b</em>.</p>\n83:  *\n84:  * <p><em>a</em> = <em>wb</em>/sqrt(<em>b</em>^2 - <em>h</em>^2).</p>\n85:  *\n86:  * <p>But <em>b</em> was defined in terms of the height of the rectangle plus\n87:  *   agreed padding at the top, so we can write.</p>\n88:  *\n89:  * <p><em>a</em> = (<em>wh</em> + <em>wb</em>)/\n90:  *                 sqrt(2<em>hp</em> + <em>p</em>^2)</p>\n91:  *\n92:  * <p>Given we now know <em>a</em> and <em>b</em>, we can find the coordinates\n93:  *   of any partition line required between use case name and extension\n94:  *   points.</p>\n95:  *\n96:  * <p>Finally we need to transform our coordinates, to recognise that the\n97:  *   origin is at our top left corner, and the Y coordinates are reversed.</p>\n98:  */\n99: \n100: public class FigUseCase extends FigNodeModelElement {\n101: \n102: \n103:     ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////\n104:     //\n105:     // Constants\n106:     //\n107:     ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////\n108: \n109:     /**\n110:      * <p>The minimum padding allowed above and below the rectangle for the use\n111:      *   case name and extension points to the top of the use case oval\n112:      *   itself.</p> */\n113: \n114:     protected final int _MIN_VERT_PADDING = 4;\n115: \n116: \n117:     /**\n118:      * <p>Space above and below the line separating name from extension\n119:      *   points. The line takes a further 1 pixel.</p>\n120:      */\n121: \n122:     protected final int _SPACER = 2;\n123: \n124: \n125:     ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////\n126:     //\n127:     // Instance variables\n128:     //\n129:     ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////\n130: \n131:     /**\n132:      * <p>UML use cases do not really have ports, so just define one big one\n133:      *   so that users can drag edges to or from any point in the icon.</p>\n134:      */\n135: \n136:     protected FigMyCircle _bigPort;\n137: \n138: \n139:     /** \n140:      * <p>We don't use _bigPort for the actual graphics of the oval. We define\n141:      *   an identical oval that sits on top of it.</p>\n142:      */\n143: \n144:     protected FigMyCircle _cover;\n145: \n146: \n147:     /** \n148:      * <p>The line separating name and extension points.</p>\n149:      */\n150: \n151:     protected FigLine _epSep;\n152: \n153: \n154:     /**\n155:      * <p>The vector of graphics for extension points (if any). First one is\n156:      *   the rectangle for the entire extension points box.</p> */\n157: \n158:     protected FigGroup _epVec;\n159: \n160: \n161:     /**\n162:      * <p>The rectangle for the entire extension point box.</p>\n163:      */\n164: \n165:     protected FigRect _epBigPort;\n166: \n167: \n168:     /**\n169:      * <p>Text highlighted by mouse actions on the diagram. Assumed to belong\n170:      *   to the extension point compartment.</p>\n171:      */\n172: \n173:     protected CompartmentFigText _highlightedFigText = null;\n174: \n175: \n176:     ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////\n177:     //\n178:     // Constructors\n179:     //\n180:     ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////\n181: \n182:     /**\n183:      * <p>Constructor for a new use case fig. We work out the smallest oval\n184:      *   that will fit round.</p>\n185:      *\n186:      * <p>At creation the extension point box is not showing (for consistency\n187:      *   with existing implementations). We can show it later.</p> */\n188: \n189:      public FigUseCase() {\n190: \n191:          // Create all the things we need, then use getMinimumSize to work out\n192:          // the dimensions of the oval.\n193: \n194:          // First the main port ellipse and the cover of identical size that\n195:          // will realize it. Use arbitrary dimensions for now.\n196: \n197:          _bigPort = new FigMyCircle(0, 0, 100, 60, Color.black, Color.white);\n198:          _cover   = new FigMyCircle(0, 0, 100, 60, Color.black, Color.white);\n199: \n200:          // Mark the text, but not the box as filled, mark that the name may\n201:          // use multiline text (a bit odd - how do we enter a multi-line\n202:          // name?).\n203: \n204:          _name.setTextFilled(false);\n205:          _name.setFilled(false);\n206:          _name.setLineWidth(0);\n207:          _name.setMultiLine(true);\n208: \n209:          // The separator, again with arbitrary bounds for now.\n210: \n211:          _epSep = new FigLine(0, 30, 100, 100, Color.black);\n212: \n213:          _epSep.setDisplayed(false);\n214: \n215:          // The surrounding box for the extension points, again with arbitrary\n216:          // bounds for now (but made the same width as the name field, so the\n217:          // name field width will dominate size calculations, but there is a\n218:          // space to double click in for a new EP. It is not filled\n219:          // (although we have to specify a fill color at creation), nor has it\n220:          // a surrounding line. Its bounds, which allow for one line (which is\n221:          // empty) are the same as for the name box at this stage.\n222:          \n223:          _epBigPort = new FigRect(0, 30, _name.getBounds().width, 20,\n224:                                   Color.black, Color.white);\n225: \n226:          _epBigPort.setFilled(false);\n227:          _epBigPort.setLineWidth(0);\n228:          _epBigPort.setDisplayed(false);\n229: \n230:          // The group for the extension points. The first entry in the vector\n231:          // is the overall surrounding box itself. The group is not filled, nor\n232:          // has any line. The first entry we add is the epBigPort\n233: \n234:          _epVec = new FigGroup();\n235: \n236:          _epVec.setFilled(false);\n237:          _epVec.setLineWidth(0);\n238:          _epVec.setDisplayed(false);\n239: \n240:          _epVec.addFig(_epBigPort);\n241: \n242:          // We now use getMiniumSize to work out the dimensions of the ellipse\n243:          // that we need, and then reset the bounds of everything.\n244: \n245:          Dimension ellipse = getMinimumSize();\n246: \n247:          // The size of the port and cover ellipses\n248: \n249:          _bigPort.setBounds(0, 0, ellipse.width, ellipse.height);\n250:          _cover.setBounds(0, 0, ellipse.width, ellipse.height);\n251: \n252:          // Space for the name. Centred horizontally, and (since we are minimum\n253:          // size) _MIN_VERT_PADDING from the top.\n254: \n255:          Dimension nameSize = _name.getMinimumSize();\n256: \n257:          _name.setBounds((ellipse.width - nameSize.width)/2, _MIN_VERT_PADDING,\n258:                          nameSize.width, nameSize.height);\n259: \n260:          // The separator. We cheat here. Since the name and extension points\n261:          // rectangles are the same size at this stage, this must be at the\n262:          // midpoint of the elipse.\n263: \n264:          _epSep.setShape(0, ellipse.height/2, ellipse.width, ellipse.height/2);\n265: \n266:          // The surrounding box for the extension points. At this stage we know\n267:          // the separator is 1 pixel wide at the midpoint, and there is _SPACER\n268:          // below this before the extension box.\n269: \n270:          Dimension epSize = _epBigPort.getMinimumSize();\n271: \n272:          _epBigPort.setBounds((ellipse.width - epSize.width)/2,\n273:                               ellipse.height/2 + 1 + _SPACER, epSize.width,\n274:                               epSize.height);\n275: \n276:          // add Figs to the FigNode in back-to-front order\n277: \n278:          addFig(_bigPort);\n279:          addFig(_cover);\n280:          addFig(_name);\n281:          addFig(_epSep);\n282:          addFig(_epVec);\n283: \n284:          // Having built the figure, getBounds finds the enclosing rectangle,\n285:          // which we set as our bounds.\n286: \n287:          Rectangle r = getBounds();\n288:          setBounds(r.x, r.y, r.width, r.height);\n289:      }\n290: \n291: \n292:     /**\n293:      * <p>A version of the constructor used to associated the Fig with a\n294:      *   particular NSUML object.</p>\n295:      *\n296:      * <p>Not clear that this is ever used. The load routines use the main\n297:      *   constructor and call setOwner directly.</p>\n298:      *\n299:      * @param gm    The graph model to associate with this Fig. Ignored in this\n300:      *              implementation.\n301:      *\n302:      * @param node  The NSUML object to associate with this Fig.\n303:      */\n304: \n305:     public FigUseCase(GraphModel gm, Object node) {\n306:         this();\n307:         setOwner(node);\n308:     }\n309: \n310: \n311:     /**\n312:      * <p>The text string to be used as the default name of the new use case\n313:      *   fig. However this seems in general to be immediately overwritten -\n314:      *   presumably somewhere in the creation code for the object, which choses\n315:      *   to define a name.</p>\n316:      *\n317:      * <p><em>Note</em>. Good UML would probably prefer a name starting with a\n318:      *   capital and no spaces!</p>\n319:      *\n320:      * @return  The desired text of the default name.\n321:      */\n322: \n323:     public String placeString() {\n324:         return \"new Use Case\";\n325:     }\n326: \n327: \n328:     /**\n329:      * <p>Make a copy of the current fig.</p>\n330:      *\n331:      * <p>Uses the generic superclass clone which gives a vector of all the\n332:      *   figs. Then initialize our instance variables from this vector.</p>\n333:      *\n334:      * @return  A new copy of the the current fig.\n335:      */\n336: \n337:     public Object clone() {\n338:         FigUseCase figClone = (FigUseCase) super.clone();\n339:         Vector     allFigs  = figClone.getFigs();\n340: \n341:         figClone._bigPort = (FigMyCircle) allFigs.elementAt(0);\n342:         figClone._cover   = (FigMyCircle) allFigs.elementAt(1);\n343:         figClone._name    = (FigText) allFigs.elementAt(2);\n344:         figClone._epSep   = (FigLine) allFigs.elementAt(3);\n345:         figClone._epVec   = (FigGroup) allFigs.elementAt(4);\n346:         \n347:         return figClone;\n348:     }\n349: \n350: \n351:     ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////\n352:     //\n353:     // Fig accessors\n354:     //\n355:     ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////\n356: \n357:     /**\n358:      * <p>Build a collection of menu items relevant for a right-click popup\n359:      *   menu on a Use Case.</p>\n360:      *\n361:      * <p>Adds to the generic pop up items from the parent.</p>\n362:      *\n363:      * @param me  The mouse event that generated this popup.\n364:      *\n365:      * @return    A collection of menu items\n366:      */\n367: \n368:     public Vector getPopUpActions(MouseEvent me) {\n369: \n370:         // Get the parent vector first\n371: \n372:         Vector popUpActions = super.getPopUpActions(me);\n373: \n374:         // Add menu to add an extension point or note. Placed one before last,\n375:         // so the \"Properties\" entry is always last.\n376: \n377:         JMenu addMenu = new JMenu(\"Add\");\n378: \n379:         addMenu.add(ActionAddExtensionPoint.singleton());\n380:         addMenu.add(ActionAddNote.SINGLETON);\n381: \n382:         popUpActions.insertElementAt(addMenu, popUpActions.size() - 1);\n383: \n384:         // Show menu to display/hide the extension point compartment. Placed\n385:         // one before last, so the \"Properties\" entry is always last.\n386: \n387:         JMenu showMenu = new JMenu(\"Show\");\n388: \n389:         if (_epVec.isDisplayed()) {\n390:             showMenu.add(ActionCompartmentDisplay.HideExtPointCompartment);\n391:         }\n392:         else {\n393:             showMenu.add(ActionCompartmentDisplay.ShowExtPointCompartment);\n394:         }\n395: \n396:         popUpActions.insertElementAt(showMenu, popUpActions.size() - 1);\n397: \n398:         // Modifier menu. Placed one before last, so the \"Properties\" entry is\n399:         // always last.\n400: \n401:         ArgoJMenu modifierMenu = new ArgoJMenu(\"Modifiers\");\n402: \n403:         MUseCase useCase = (MUseCase) getOwner();\n404: \n405:         modifierMenu.addCheckItem(new ActionModifier(\"Abstract\",\n406:                                                      \"isAbstract\",\n407:                                                      \"isAbstract\",\n408:                                                      \"setAbstract\",\n409:                                                      useCase));\n410: \n411:         modifierMenu.addCheckItem(new ActionModifier(\"Leaf\",\n412:                                                      \"isLeaf\",\n413:                                                      \"isLeaf\",\n414:                                                      \"setLeaf\",\n415:                                                      useCase));\n416: \n417:         modifierMenu.addCheckItem(new ActionModifier(\"Root\",\n418:                                                      \"isRoot\",\n419:                                                      \"isRoot\",\n420:                                                      \"setRoot\",\n421:                                                      useCase));\n422: \n423:         popUpActions.insertElementAt(modifierMenu, popUpActions.size() - 1);\n424: \n425:         return popUpActions;\n426:     }\n427: \n428: \n429:     /**\n430:      * <p>Returns whether the extension points are currently displayed.</p>\n431:      *\n432:      * @return  <code>true</code> if the attributes are visible,\n433:      *          <code>false</code> otherwise.\n434:      */\n435: \n436:     public boolean isExtensionPointVisible() {\n437:         return _epVec.isDisplayed();\n438:     }\n439: \n440: \n441:     /**\n442:      * <p>Set the visibility of the extension point compartment. This is called\n443:      *   from outside this class when the user sets visibility explicitly\n444:      *   through the style panel or the context sensitive pop-up menu.</p>\n445:      *\n446:      * <p>We don't change the size of the use case, so we just have to mark the\n447:      *   extension point elements' visibility. {@link #setBounds(int, int,\n448:      *   int, int)} will do the relayout (with name in the middle) for us.</p>\n449:      *\n450:      * @param isVisible  <code>true</code> if the compartment should be shown,\n451:      *                   <code>false</code> otherwise.\n452:      */\n453: \n454:     public void setExtensionPointVisible(boolean isVisible) {\n455: \n456:         // Record our current bounds for later use\n457: \n458:         Rectangle oldBounds = getBounds();\n459: \n460:         // First case is where the extension points are currently displayed and\n461:         // we are asked to turn them off.\n462: \n463:         if (_epVec.isDisplayed() & (!isVisible)) {\n464: \n465:             // Tell GEF that we are starting to make a change. Loop through the\n466:             // epVec marking each element as not visible.\n467: \n468:             startTrans();\n469: \n470:             Enumeration enum = _epVec.getFigs().elements();\n471: \n472:             while (enum.hasMoreElements()) {\n473:                 ((Fig)(enum.nextElement())).setDisplayed(false);\n474:             }\n475: \n476:             // Mark the vector itself and the separator as not displayed\n477: \n478:             _epVec.setDisplayed(false);\n479:             _epSep.setDisplayed(false);\n480: \n481:             // Redo the bounds and then tell GEF the change has finished\n482: \n483:             setBounds(oldBounds.x, oldBounds.y, oldBounds.width,\n484:                       oldBounds.height);\n485:             endTrans();\n486:         }\n487: \n488:         // Second case is where the extension points are not currently\n489:         // displayed and we are asked to turn them on.\n490: \n491:         else if ((!_epVec.isDisplayed()) & isVisible) {\n492: \n493:             // Tell GEF that we are starting to make a change. Loop through the\n494:             // epVec marking each element as visible.\n495: \n496:             startTrans();\n497: \n498:             Enumeration enum = _epVec.getFigs().elements();\n499: \n500:             while (enum.hasMoreElements()) {\n501:                 ((Fig)(enum.nextElement())).setDisplayed(true);\n502:             }\n503: \n504:             // Mark the vector itself and the separator as displayed\n505: \n506:             _epVec.setDisplayed(true);\n507:             _epSep.setDisplayed(true);\n508: \n509:             // Redo the bounds and then tell GEF the change has finished\n510: \n511:             setBounds(oldBounds.x, oldBounds.y, oldBounds.width,\n512:                       oldBounds.height);\n513:             endTrans();\n514:         }\n515:     }\n516: \n517: \n518:     /**\n519:      * <p>Creates a set of handles for dragging generalization/specializations\n520:      *   or associations.</p>\n521:      *\n522:      * @return  The new selection object (a GEF entity).\n523:      */\n524: \n525:     public Selection makeSelection() {\n526:         return new SelectionUseCase(this);\n527:     }\n528: \n529: \n530:     /**\n531:      * <p>Associate this fig with a particular NSUML object.</p>\n532:      *\n533:      * <p>Associates the node with the \"bigPort\" that is the whole of this\n534:      *   object.</p>\n535:      *\n536:      * <p>Must be public, since called directly, e.g. by the load routines.</p>\n537:      *\n538:      * @param node  The NSUML object to associate with this fig.\n539:      */\n540: \n541:     public void setOwner(Object node) {\n542:         super.setOwner(node);\n543:         bindPort(node, _bigPort);\n544:     }\n545: \n546: \n547:     /**\n548:      * <p>Compute the minimum acceptable size of the use case.</p>\n549:      *\n550:      * <p>We work out the minimum size of the text box, and from that the radii\n551:      *   of the enclosing ellipse.</p>\n552:      *\n553:      * @return  The dimensions of the smallest size bounding box of the use\n554:      *          case.\n555:      */\n556: \n557:      public Dimension getMinimumSize() {\n558: \n559:          Dimension textSize = _getTextSize();\n560: \n561:          Dimension _size = _calcEllipse(textSize, _MIN_VERT_PADDING);\n562: \n563:          return new Dimension(Math.max(_size.width,100), Math.max(_size.height,60));\n564:      }\n565: \n566: \n567:     /**\n568:      * <p>A private utility routine to calculate the minimum size of the\n569:      *   rectangle to hold the name and extension points (if displayed).</p>\n570:      *\n571:      * @return  The dimensions of the rectangle\n572:      */\n573: \n574:     private Dimension _getTextSize() {\n575:          Dimension minSize = _name.getMinimumSize();\n576: \n577:          // Now allow for the extension points, if they are displayed\n578: \n579:          if (_epVec.isDisplayed()) {\n580: \n581:              // Allow for a separator (spacer each side + 1 pixel width line)\n582: \n583:              minSize.height += 2*_SPACER + 1;\n584: \n585:              // Loop through all the extension points, to find the widest\n586:              // (remember the first fig is the box for the whole lot, so ignore\n587:              // it).\n588: \n589:             Enumeration enum = _epVec.getFigs().elements();\n590:             enum.nextElement();  // ignore\n591: \n592:             while (enum.hasMoreElements()) {\n593:                 int elemWidth =\n594:                     ((FigText)enum.nextElement()).getMinimumSize().width;\n595:                 minSize.width = Math.max(minSize.width, elemWidth);\n596:             }\n597: \n598:             // Height allows one row for each extension point (remember to\n599:             // ignore the first element, which is the box for the lot), subject\n600:             // to there always being space for at least one extension point.\n601:                                        \n602:             minSize.height += ROWHEIGHT * \n603:                               Math.max(1, _epVec.getFigs().size() - 1);\n604:          }\n605: \n606:          return minSize;\n607:     }\n608: \n609: \n610:     /**\n611:      * <p>A private utility to calculate the bounding oval for the given\n612:      *   rectangular text box.</p>\n613:      *\n614:      * <p>To sufficiently constrain the problem, we define that there is a gap\n615:      *   given by the parameter <code>vertPadding</code> above the top of the\n616:      *   box to the top of the oval.</p>\n617:      *\n618:      * <p>All computations are done in double, and then converted to integer at\n619:      *   the end.</p>\n620:      *\n621:      * @param rectSize     The dimensions of the rectangle to be bounded\n622:      *\n623:      * @param vertPadding  The padding between the top of the box and the top\n624:      *                     of the ellipse.\n625:      *\n626:      * @return             The dimensions of the required oval */\n627: \n628:     private Dimension _calcEllipse(Dimension rectSize, int vertPadding) {\n629: \n630:         // Work out the radii of the ellipse, a and b. The top right corner of\n631:         // the ellipse (Cartesian coordinates, centred on the origin) will be\n632:         // at (x,y)\n633: \n634:         double a;\n635:         double b = ((double) rectSize.height)/2.0 + (double) vertPadding;\n636: \n637:         double x = ((double) rectSize.width)/2.0;\n638:         double y = ((double) rectSize.height)/2.0;\n639: \n640:         // Formula for a is described in the overall class description.\n641: \n642:         a = (x*b)/Math.sqrt(b*b - y*y);\n643: \n644:         // Result as integers, rounded up. We ensure that the radii are\n645:         // integers for convenience.\n646: \n647:         return new Dimension(((int) (Math.ceil(a))*2),\n648:                              ((int) (Math.ceil(b))*2));\n649:     }\n650:         \n651: \n652:     /**\n653:      * <p>Change the boundary of the use case.</p>\n654:      *\n655:      * <p>If we are called with less than the minimum size, we impose the\n656:      *   minimum size.</p>\n657:      *\n658:      * <p>We place the name and extension points at the centre of the\n659:      *   rectangle.</p>\n660:      *\n661:      * <p>Set the bounds of all components of the Fig.</p>\n662:      *\n663:      * @param x  X coordinate of upper left corner\n664:      *\n665:      * @param y  Y coordinate of upper left corner\n666:      *\n667:      * @param w  width of bounding box\n668:      *\n669:      * @param h  height of bounding box\n670:      */\n671: \n672:     public void setBounds(int x, int y, int w, int h) {\n673: \n674:         // Remember where we are at present, so we can tell GEF later. Then\n675:         // check we are as big as the minimum size\n676: \n677:         Rectangle oldBounds = getBounds();\n678:         Dimension minSize   = getMinimumSize();\n679: \n680:         int newW = (minSize.width  > w) ? minSize.width  : w;\n681:         int newH = (minSize.height > h) ? minSize.height : h;\n682: \n683:         // Work out the size of the name and extension point rectangle, and\n684:         // hence the vertical padding\n685: \n686:         Dimension textSize = _getTextSize();\n687:         int       vPadding = (newH - textSize.height)/2;\n688: \n689:         // Adjust the alignment of the name.\n690: \n691:         Dimension nameSize = _name.getMinimumSize();\n692: \n693:         _name.setBounds(x + ((newW - nameSize.width)/2), y + vPadding,\n694:                         nameSize.width, nameSize.height);\n695: \n696:         // Place extension points if they are showing\n697: \n698:         if (_epVec.isDisplayed()) {\n699: \n700:             // currY tracks the current vertical position of each element. The\n701:             // separator is _SPACER pixels below the name. Its length is\n702:             // calculated from the formula for an ellipse.\n703: \n704:             int currY  = y + vPadding + nameSize.height + _SPACER;\n705:             int sepLen = 2 * (int) (_calcX(((double) newW)/2.0,\n706:                                            ((double) newH)/2.0,\n707:                                            ((double) newH)/2.0 -\n708:                                              ((double) (currY - y))));\n709: \n710:             _epSep.setShape(x + (newW - sepLen)/2, currY,\n711:                             x + (newW + sepLen)/2, currY);\n712: \n713:             // Extension points are 1 pixel for the line and _SPACER gap below\n714:             // the separator\n715: \n716:             currY += 1 + _SPACER;\n717: \n718:             // Use the utility routine getUpdatedSize to move the extension\n719:             // point figures. We can discard the result of this routine. For\n720:             // now we assume that extension points are the width of the overall\n721:             // text rectangle (true unless the name is wider than any EP).\n722: \n723:             Dimension epSize =\n724:                 getUpdatedSize(_epVec, x + ((newW - textSize.width)/2),\n725:                                currY, textSize.width,\n726:                                textSize.height - nameSize.height -\n727:                                  _SPACER*2 - 1); \n728:         }\n729: \n730:         // Set the bounds of the bigPort and cover\n731: \n732:         _bigPort.setBounds(x, y, newW, newH);\n733:         _cover.setBounds(x, y, newW, newH);\n734: \n735:         // Record the changes in the instance variables of our parent, tell GEF\n736:         // and trigger the edges to reconsider themselves.\n737: \n738:         _x = x;\n739:         _y = y;\n740:         _w = newW;\n741:         _h = newH;\n742:         \n743:         firePropChange(\"bounds\", oldBounds, getBounds());\n744:         updateEdges();\n745:     }\n746:         \n747: \n748:     /**\n749:      * <p>Private utility routine to work out the (positive) x coordinate of a\n750:      *   point on an oval, given the radii and y coordinate.</p>\n751:      *\n752:      * @param a  radius in X direction\n753:      *\n754:      * @param b  radius in Y direction\n755:      *\n756:      * @param y  Y coordinate\n757:      *\n758:      * @return   Positive X coordinate for the given Y coordinate\n759:      */\n760: \n761:     private double _calcX(double a, double b, double y) {\n762:         return (a*Math.sqrt(b*b - y*y))/b;\n763:     }\n764: \n765: \n766:     /**\n767:      * <p>Set the line colour for the use case oval.</p>\n768:      *\n769:      * <p>This involves setting the _cover oval, not the bigPort.</p>\n770:      *\n771:      * @param col The colour desired.  */\n772: \n773:     public void setLineColor(Color col) {\n774:         _cover.setLineColor(col);\n775:     }\n776: \n777: \n778:     /**\n779:      * <p>Get the line colour for the use case oval.</p>\n780:      *\n781:      * <p>This involves getting the _cover oval colour, not the bigPort.</p>\n782:      *\n783:      * @return  The colour in use.\n784:      */\n785: \n786:     public Color getLineColor() {\n787:         return _cover.getLineColor();\n788:     }\n789: \n790: \n791:     /**\n792:      * <p>Set the fill colour for the use case oval.</p>\n793:      *\n794:      * <p>This involves setting the _cover oval, not the bigPort.</p>\n795:      *\n796:      * @param col  The colour desired.\n797:      */\n798: \n799:     public void setFillColor(Color col) {\n800:         _cover.setFillColor(col);\n801:     }\n802: \n803: \n804:     /**\n805:      * <p>Get the line colour for the use case oval.</p>\n806:      *\n807:      * <p>This involves getting the _cover oval colour, not the bigPort.</p>\n808:      *\n809:      * @return  The colour in use.\n810:      */\n811: \n812:     public Color getFillColor() {\n813:         return _cover.getFillColor();\n814:     }\n815: \n816: \n817:     /**\n818:      * <p>Set whether the use case oval is to be filled.</p>\n819:      *\n820:      * <p>This involves setting the _cover oval, not the bigPort.</p>\n821:      *\n822:      * @param f  <code>true</code> if the oval is to be filled,\n823:      *           <code>false</code> if not.\n824:      */\n825: \n826:     public void setFilled(boolean f) {\n827:         _cover.setFilled(f);\n828:     }\n829: \n830: \n831:     /**\n832:      * <p>Get whether the use case oval is to be filled.</p>\n833:      *\n834:      * <p>This involves getting the _cover oval, not the bigPort.</p>\n835:      *\n836:      * @return  <code>true</code> if the oval is to be filled,\n837:      *          <code>false</code> if not.\n838:      */\n839: \n840:     public boolean getFilled() {\n841:         return _cover.getFilled();\n842:     }\n843: \n844: \n845:     /**\n846:      * <p>Set the line width for the use case oval.</p>\n847:      *\n848:      * <p>This involves setting the _cover oval, not the bigPort.</p>\n849:      *\n850:      * @param w  The line width desired.\n851:      */\n852: \n853:     public void setLineWidth(int w) {\n854:         _cover.setLineWidth(w);\n855:     }\n856: \n857: \n858:     /**\n859:      * <p>Get the line width for the use case oval.</p>\n860:      *\n861:      * <p>This involves getting the _cover oval colour, not the bigPort.</p>\n862:      *\n863:      * @return  The line width set.\n864:      */\n865: \n866:     public int getLineWidth() {\n867:         return _cover.getLineWidth();\n868:     }\n869: \n870:     \n871:     /**\n872:      * <p>FigMyCircle is a FigCircle with corrected connectionPoint method:\n873:      *   this methods calculates where a connected edge ends.</p>\n874:      */\n875: \n876:     public class FigMyCircle extends FigCircle {\n877:         protected Category cat = Category.getInstance(FigMyCircle.class);\n878: \n879:         /**\n880:          * <p>Constructor just invokes the parent constructor.</p>\n881:          *\n882:          * @param x       X coordinate of the upper left corner of the bounding\n883:          *                box.\n884:          *\n885:          * @param y       Y coordinate of the upper left corner of the bounding\n886:          *                box.\n887:          *\n888:          * @param w       Width of the bounding box.\n889:          *\n890:          * @param h       Height of the bounding box.\n891:          *\n892:          * @param lColor  Line colour of the fig.\n893:          *\n894:          * @param fColor  Fill colour of the fig.\n895:          */\n896: \n897:         public FigMyCircle(int x, int y, int w, int h, Color lColor,\n898:                            Color fColor) {\n899:             super(x, y, w, h, lColor, fColor);\n900:         }\n901: \n902: \n903:         /**\n904:          * <p>Compute the border point of the elipse that is on the edge\n905:          *   between the stored upper left corner and the given parameter.</p>\n906:          *\n907:          * @param anotherPt  The remote point to which an edge is drawn.\n908:          *\n909:          * @return           The connection point on the boundary of the\n910:          *                   elipse.\n911:          */\n912: \n913:         public Point connectionPoint(Point anotherPt) {\n914:             double rx = _w/2;\n915:             double ry = _h/2;\n916:             double dx = anotherPt.x - _x;\n917:             double dy = anotherPt.y - _y;\n918:             double dd = ry*ry*dx*dx + rx*rx*dy*dy;\n919:             double mu = rx*ry/Math.sqrt(dd);\n920: \n921:             Point res = new Point((int)(mu*dx+_x+rx),(int)(mu*dy+_y+ry));\n922:             cat.debug(\"    returns \"+res.x+','+res.y+')');\n923:             return res;\n924:         }\n925:     }\n926: \n927:     ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////\n928:     //\n929:     // Event handlers\n930:     //\n931:     ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////\n932: \n933: \n934:     /**\n935:      * <p>React to a mouse key being pressed.</p>\n936:      *\n937:      * @param me  The mouse action that caused us to be invoked.\n938:      */\n939: \n940:     public void mousePressed(MouseEvent me) {\n941: \n942:         // Deal with anything from the parent first\n943: \n944:         super.mousePressed(me);\n945: \n946:         // If we are currently selected, turn off the draggable buttons at each\n947:         // side, and unhighlight any currently selected extension points.\n948: \n949:         Editor    ce  = Globals.curEditor();\n950:         Selection sel = ce.getSelectionManager().findSelectionFor(this);\n951: \n952:         if (sel instanceof SelectionUseCase) {\n953:             ((SelectionUseCase) sel).hideButtons();\n954:         }\n955: \n956:         unhighlight();\n957: \n958:         // Display extension point properties if necessary. Look to see if the\n959:         // mouse (2x2 pixels) hit the extension point compartment. Use a flag\n960:         // to track this.\n961: \n962:         Rectangle r = new Rectangle(me.getX() - 1, me.getY() - 1, 2, 2);\n963:         Fig       f = hitFig(r);\n964: \n965:         boolean targetIsSet = false;\n966: \n967:         if (f == _epVec) {\n968: \n969:             // Work out which extension point this corresponds to. Each EP\n970:             // takes ROWHEIGHT pixels, so take the difference between the\n971:             // centre of the mouse (me.getY() - 1) and the top of the epVec\n972:             // (f.getY()) and integer divide by ROWHEIGHT.\n973: \n974:             Vector v = _epVec.getFigs();\n975:             int    i = (me.getY() - f.getY() - 1)/ROWHEIGHT;\n976: \n977:             // If we are in the range of the EP list size (avoids any nasty\n978:             // boundary overflows), we can select that EP entry. Make this\n979:             // entry the target Fig, and note that we do have a\n980:             // target. Remember that the first entry in the vector is the\n981:             // bigPort itself.\n982: \n983:             if ((i >= 0) && (i < (v.size() - 1))) {\n984:                 targetIsSet = true;\n985:                 f           = (Fig)v.elementAt(i + 1);\n986: \n987: \t\t_highlightedFigText = (CompartmentFigText)f;\n988: \t\t_highlightedFigText.setHighlighted(true);\n989:             }\n990: \t}\n991: \n992:         // If we didn't get the EP compartment, we just select ourself.\n993: \n994: \tif (!targetIsSet) {\n995:             ProjectBrowser.TheInstance.setTarget(getOwner());\n996:         }\n997:     }\n998: \n999: \n1000:     /**\n1001:      * <p>Deal with the mouse leaving the fig. Unhighlight the fig.</p>\n1002:      *\n1003:      * @param me  The mouse action that caused us to be invoked.\n1004:      */\n1005: \n1006:     public void mouseExited(MouseEvent me) {\n1007:         super.mouseExited(me);\n1008:         unhighlight();\n1009:     }\n1010: \n1011: \n1012:     /**\n1013:      * <p>Deal with a key being pressed.</p>\n1014:      *\n1015:      * <p>We deal with UP and DOWN, and use these to move through the list\n1016:      *   of selected extension points. We deal with ENTER and use that to start\n1017:      *   the text editor.</p>\n1018:      *\n1019:      * @param ke  The key event that caused us to be invoked.\n1020:      */\n1021: \n1022:     public void keyPressed(KeyEvent ke) {\n1023:         int key = ke.getKeyCode();\n1024: \n1025:         // For UP and DOWN cycle through the extension points.\n1026: \n1027:         if ((key == KeyEvent.VK_UP) || (key == KeyEvent.VK_DOWN)) {\n1028: \n1029:             // Find the currently highlighted EP (if any) and unhighlight it\n1030: \n1031:             CompartmentFigText ft = unhighlight();\n1032: \n1033:             if (ft != null) {\n1034:                 int i = _epVec.getFigs().indexOf(ft);\n1035: \n1036:                 // If we found one of the current EP's move forward or\n1037:                 // backwards as appopriate, and set a newly selected EP as\n1038:                 // highlighted\n1039: \n1040:                 if (i != -1) {\n1041:                     if (key == KeyEvent.VK_UP) {\n1042:                         ft = getPreviousVisibleFeature(_epVec, i);\n1043:                     } else {\n1044:                         ft = getNextVisibleFeature(_epVec, i);\n1045:                     }\n1046: \n1047:                     if (ft != null) {\n1048:                         ft.setHighlighted(true);\n1049:                         _highlightedFigText = ft;\n1050:                         return;\n1051:                     }\n1052:                 }\n1053:             }\n1054:         }\n1055: \n1056:         // For ENTER start editing any text that is highlighted, remembering to\n1057:         // consume the ENTER event.\n1058: \n1059:         else if ((key == KeyEvent.VK_ENTER) && (_highlightedFigText != null)) {\n1060:             _highlightedFigText.startTextEditor(ke);\n1061:             ke.consume();\n1062:             return;\n1063:         }\n1064: \n1065:         // Anything else defer to our parent.\n1066: \n1067:         else {\n1068:             super.keyPressed(ke);\n1069:         }\n1070:     }\n1071: \n1072: \n1073:     ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////\n1074:     //\n1075:     // Internal methods\n1076:     //\n1077:     ///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////\n1078: \n1079: \n1080:     /**\n1081:      * <p>Invoked when text has been edited.</p>\n1082:      *\n1083:      * <p>We check that it is one of the extension point compartments and then\n1084:      *  parse accordingly.</p>\n1085:      *\n1086:      * @param ft  The text that has been edited.\n1087:      */\n1088: \n1089:     protected void textEdited(FigText ft) throws PropertyVetoException {\n1090: \n1091:         // Let the parent do anything it wants first\n1092: \n1093:         super.textEdited(ft);\n1094: \n1095:         // Only works if we have an owner\n1096: \n1097:         MUseCase useCase = (MUseCase) getOwner();\n1098: \n1099:         if (useCase == null) {\n1100:             return;\n1101:         }\n1102: \n1103:         // Give up if we are not one of the extension points\n1104: \n1105:         int i = _epVec.getFigs().indexOf(ft);\n1106: \n1107:         if (i == -1) {\n1108:             return;\n1109:         }\n1110: \n1111:         // Mark the text as highlighted, then parse it\n1112: \n1113:         CompartmentFigText highlightedFigText = (CompartmentFigText)ft;\n1114:         highlightedFigText.setHighlighted(true);\n1115: \n1116:         MExtensionPoint ep   = \n1117:             (MExtensionPoint) (highlightedFigText.getOwner());\n1118:         String          text = highlightedFigText.getText().trim();\n1119:         \n1120:         ParserDisplay.SINGLETON.parseExtensionPointFig(useCase, ep, text);\n1121: \n1122:         return;\n1123:     }\n1124: \n1125: \n1126:     /**\n1127:      * <p>Private method to find the previous visible feature to highlight.</p>\n1128:      *\n1129:      * <p>We're passed in a group (which will be the extension point vector)\n1130:      *   and the currently highlighted fig (a member of that vector).</p>\n1131:      *\n1132:      * @param fgVec  A fig group (invariably the extension point vector) in\n1133:      *               which to seek the previous visible feature.\n1134:      *\n1135:      * @param i      The index of the currently selected entry in\n1136:      *               <code>fgVec</code>.\n1137:      *\n1138:      * @return       The new fig to use.\n1139:      */\n114